"The Stern–Gerlach Experiment | VirtualDemos",
"The Stern–Gerlach Experiment",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Stern and Gerlach, {#}; Silver at one speed; Mystery atom (these three use the Stern–Gerlach view, the subject of this worksheet); Orbit as a current loop; 3d electron in a field (the orbital magnetic moment worksheet); Zeeman: cadmium red line (the Zeeman effect worksheet).",
"Atoms leave an oven through two slits that shape the beam into a ribbon {#} mm high and {#} mm long. They cross a magnet {#} cm long, drift a distance \\(L_2\\) that you choose, and stick to a glass screen.",
"The magnet’s field changes steadily across the beam, at a gradient dB/dz that is the same everywhere in the gap.",
"A hot oven fills every magnetic sublevel \\(m_J\\) of the atom equally. Each sublevel has \\(\\mu_z = -g_J m_J \\mu_\\mathrm{B}\\), with \\(g_J\\) from the Landé formula and the electron’s g factor {#}. Ground levels are from the NIST Atomic Spectra Database, and masses are standard atomic weights.",
"Without the velocity selector the atoms have the spread of speeds of an effusive beam, and the reading’s speed is the one at which the deposit peaks, \\(v^2 = 6kT/M\\). With the selector on, every atom has the speed you choose.",
"Two of the atoms in the list are imagined, for comparison: an l = {#} atom with no spin, and classical magnets of moment \\(\\mu_\\mathrm{B}\\) pointing every way.",
